::: {.notes}
*Notes:* This table reports OLS estimates of @eq-employee-wage for employees in HILDA waves 1 to 21. The dependent variable is the log hourly wage in the employee's main job. Each column represents a different weighting of the leave-one-out owner shares of @eq-loo-shares, namely no decay (Type 0, $\lambda = 0$), which is our main specification, pooled decay (Type 1, $\lambda = 0.02$) and origin-specific decay (Type 2, $\lambda_{\text{MES}} = 0.04$ and $\lambda_{\text{MNES}} = 0$). Workplace types are compared with other workplaces of 20 or more people, and the owner shares run from 0 to 1. All columns include covariates for age, sex, origin group, casual or permanent employment, occupation, education, SEIFA decile, occupational tenure, employer tenure and union membership, and year, industry-year and state-year fixed effects. The F-statistic, with its degrees of freedom in parentheses, tests the null hypothesis that the three owner-share interactions are jointly zero, using the clustered covariance matrix; its p-value is reported in the row below. All standard errors reported in parentheses are clustered at the individual and industry-year levels. Full results are reported in @tbl-b1-appendix. ∗ p < 0.10, ∗∗ p < 0.05, ∗∗∗ p < 0.01.
:::

@tbl-b1-main reports OLS estimates of @eq-employee-wage on 156,786 person-years from 23,067 employees. The first column is our main specification, which uses the owner shares without decay (Type 0). The second and third columns apply the pooled and origin-specific decay weightings of @sec-assimilation and are reported for comparison.

Employees of small single-site workplaces earn 10.7 per cent less than employees of other large workplaces in the same industry and year ($\beta_1 = -0.113$),[^demo] while employees of chain-like workplaces earn about 6 per cent more ($\beta_3 = 0.060$). Small sites of multi-site employers fall in between ($\beta_2 = -0.030$).

[^demo]: The percentage difference is $100 \times (e^{\beta} - 1)$. For example, $\beta = -0.113$ corresponds to a 10.7 per cent lower wage relative to the reference category.

The small single-site wage gap narrows where owners from non-English-speaking countries with high English proficiency are more common, but not where owners with limited English are common. The interaction with the high-English MNES share is $\beta_5 = 0.277$ ($p < 0.001$), so a one-standard-deviation increase in that share (0.104) narrows the gap by 2.9 percentage points. The interaction with the low-English MNES share is $\beta_6 = -0.182$ ($p = 0.295$), negative and far from significant. The three interactions are jointly significant ($p < 0.001$).

To gauge the size of these interactions, we compute the predicted small single-site wage gap at different owner shares. Where there are no non-English-speaking migrant owners, employees of small single-site workplaces earn 10.7 per cent less than employees of large workplaces in the same industry and year. At the 95th percentile of the high-English owner share (34.7 per cent of owners), the penalty narrows to 1.7 per cent. At the 95th percentile of the low-English owner share (6.9 per cent of owners), it is 11.8 per cent, close to the penalty where there are no such owners. Working in a small independent business therefore costs employees less in industries where more such businesses are run by non-English-speaking migrants who speak English well, but not where more are run by owners with limited English. These gaps compare employees of different workplace types within the same industry and year. They are not gaps between owners and employees, nor between migrant and native-born employees.

The MES share does not behave like the native-born benchmark we expected. Its interaction is $\beta_4 = -0.330$ ($p < 0.001$), so a higher share of MES owners is associated with a *wider* small single-site gap. In terms of the explanations set out in @sec-block1, the positive high-English interaction is consistent with better job matching or with power reversal, but the absence of any narrowing where owners have limited English does not show the concentration of the effect among low-English owners that power reversal predicts.

### Robustness and Sensitivity Checks {#sec-block1-robust}

The wage estimates rest on several measurement choices and, for some origin groups, on very few owners. We assess how sensitive they are to each choice in turn; the corresponding tables are in Appendix [-@sec-appendix-b1].

*Construction of migrant owner density.* HILDA observes few MNES owners. Only 47 low-English owners appear in waves 1 to 21, and the low-English share is zero for 64.1 per cent of employees, so the share in any industry-year rests on a handful of owners and depends on how each of them is weighted. We therefore vary the weighting in two ways. The decay weightings in the second and third columns of @tbl-b1-main down-weight long-settled migrant owners, and @tbl-b1-lnw builds the shares with the longitudinal instead of the cross-sectional survey weight. The high-English interaction remains positive and significant under every decay weighting. The low-English interaction is far less stable. Under the longitudinal weight, which is positive for only 14 of the 47 low-English owners, it becomes positive ($0.456$, $p < 0.001$). The choice of weighting therefore matters for the low-English estimate, which we treat as uninformative. Sampling error in a share that rests on few owners also acts like measurement error in a regressor and biases its interaction towards zero, which reinforces this conclusion.

*Workplace size threshold.* The 20-person cut-off follows the ABS definition of a small business, but the businesses that migrants own may be smaller or larger than this. We redefine small workplaces at 10 and 50 people (@tbl-b1-robustness). The high-English interaction is $0.269$ ($p < 0.001$) and $0.255$ ($p < 0.001$) under the two thresholds, and the low-English interaction remains negative and insignificant ($-0.272$ and $-0.199$). The results do not depend on the size threshold.

*Industry classification.* The 19 ANZSIC divisions are broad, and migrant owners may concentrate in narrower industries within them. We therefore construct the owner shares and the industry-by-year fixed effects at the two-digit level (@tbl-b1-robustness-2digit). The pattern weakens. The high-English interaction falls to $0.048$ ($p = 0.081$) and the low-English interaction is $-0.110$ ($p = 0.163$), although the three interactions remain jointly significant. Two-digit shares rest on even fewer owners per cell, so the weaker estimates are consistent with greater attenuation as well as with a weaker underlying relationship.

*Migrant ownership versus English proficiency.* The three origin-specific shares are correlated, which makes it hard to separate migrant ownership from owners' English. We therefore replace them with the share of all migrant owners and the share of low-English owners (@tbl-b1v2-appendix), so that the low-English coefficient measures any effect of limited English over and above migrant ownership. The migrant share has a coefficient of $0.156$ ($p = 0.002$), while the low-English share has a coefficient of $-0.298$ ($p = 0.126$). The narrowing is therefore associated with migrant ownership in general, not with owners' limited English.

*Chain workplaces.* If the interactions reflect migrant ownership, they should appear mainly for workplaces that migrants are likely to own. Small sites of multi-site employers share the local scale of small independent workplaces but belong to larger, mostly native-run employers, so they allow us to check whether the main interactions instead reflect the pay structure of industries with many migrant owners. We add interactions of each owner share with small-site chains and with chain-like workplaces (@tbl-b1-placebo). The small-site chain interactions are jointly significant ($p = 0.014$), mainly because small sites of chains also pay relatively more where high-English MNES owners are common ($0.141$, $p = 0.002$), and the chain-like interactions are also jointly significant ($p < 0.001$). Once these terms are included, the small single-site interactions with the high-English and low-English shares are $0.280$ ($p < 0.001$) and $-0.033$ ($p = 0.841$). The high-English interaction for small independent workplaces is about twice that for small sites of chains, so the narrowing is strongest where migrants are most likely to be owners, but it is not confined to those workplaces.

@fig-b1-placebo shows what this extended model implies for every workplace type. Where no non-English-speaking migrants own businesses, employees of small single-site workplaces earn 11.9 per cent less than employees of other large workplaces, employees of small sites of chains 6.5 per cent less and employees of chain-like workplaces 5.6 per cent more. As the share of high-English MNES owners rises to its 95th percentile, these differences become -2.9, -1.8, +2.8 per cent. The wage distribution across workplace types therefore compresses in industries with many high-English MNES owners. The compression is largest for small independent workplaces, but part of the main pattern reflects the pay structure of these industries and not ownership alone.


::: {.cell}
