Among the native-born, running a small business with employees involves a clear trade-off between income and risk. In the unrestricted sample, small employers earn \$12,730 more per year than employees in small workplaces ($p < 0.001$), but the average year-to-year change in their income is 27.3 percentage points larger and their probability of an income fall of 25 per cent or more is 15.1 points higher.

The same trade-off holds for every migrant group. Small employers earn more than employees of their own origin, and in every group they face much higher volatility and downside risk. In the unrestricted sample the income gap is \$17,217 for high-English migrants ($p < 0.001$) and \$24,541 for low-English migrants ($p = 0.017$), while for MES migrants it is smaller and only marginally significant (\$9,263, $p = 0.083$). Ownership with employees therefore looks like compensated risk for all groups, not the uncompensated risk that the power-reversal hypothesis predicts for owners with limited English.

None of the differences from the native-born gap for low-English small employers is significant (@tbl-b2-premium). Their risk gaps point in the predicted direction, with volatility 11.8 percentage points higher and the probability of a large fall 4.4 points higher than the native-born comparison would imply, but the income premium gap is positive (\$11,810), not negative. With 21 low-English small employers in the unrestricted sample and 13 in the restricted sample, these estimates are very imprecise. The minimum detectable differences are \$29,863 for income, 34.4 percentage points for volatility and 19.8 percentage points for downside risk, so the data cannot rule out large differences in either direction. High-English small employers, if anything, face less volatility than the native-born comparison would imply (−5.5 points, $p = 0.054$), and MES small employers are close to their native-born counterparts on every outcome.

### Heterogeneity by Owner Type {#sec-block2-het}

@tbl-b2-large and @tbl-b2-ownacc report the same comparisons for large employers and own-account workers. Large migrant employers are too few for firm conclusions. Their income estimates are dominated by a handful of very high incomes, with a standard error of \$126,299 for the difference between MES and native-born large employers, so the data cannot show whether migrant-specific gaps are larger in small than in large businesses. Own-account workers earn less than employees of their own origin in every group, although not significantly so for every migrant group (−\$9,379 among the native-born in the unrestricted sample), and face even more volatility than small employers. The joint test that all origin by owner-type interactions are zero is marginally significant for volatility in the restricted sample ($p = 0.055$), but not in the unrestricted sample ($p = 0.182$), nor for income or downside risk in either sample. As @tbl-b2-ownacc shows, the restricted-sample result comes mainly from own-account workers.

Low-English own-account workers stand out. In the restricted sample, their income appears much less volatile than the native-born comparison implies (−19.1 percentage points, $p = 0.056$). In the unrestricted sample, which also includes those who became own-account workers between the two years, the difference is smaller and not significant (−10.7 points, $p = 0.300$). The apparent protection from risk is therefore concentrated among the 13 low-English own-account workers who stay in that status in both years.

### Robustness and Sensitivity Checks {#sec-block2-robust}

*Changes of status between waves.* HILDA measures income over the financial year before the interview, so for people who changed status between interviews, income at $t$ partly reflects their earlier status. We therefore estimate every model on both samples. Restricting the sample to individuals whose status is unchanged gives larger native-born gaps, of \$14,369 in income, 32.7 points in volatility and 17.3 points in downside risk, but the same pattern across origin groups. The comparison between owners and employees is therefore not driven by people who changed status between the two years.

*Attrition.* Owners who are not re-interviewed at $t$ drop out of both samples, which would bias the comparisons if attrition differed by origin. Attrition is similar across groups, at 5 to 8 per cent a year among small employers (@tbl-b2-cells), so it is unlikely to explain the results. Neither sample, however, measures the income consequences of a business closing.

*Hours of work.* Annual income does not account for the longer hours that owners typically work. We therefore compare the median hourly income of owners with employees with the median hourly wage of employees of small independent workplaces, by industry and without controls (@sec-appendix-gap). Owners earn less per hour than employees in several low-margin industries, such as accommodation and food services, so the income premium in @tbl-b2-main does not hold in every industry once hours are taken into account. The full regression estimates, including all controls, are in @tbl-b2-appendix.

## Entry into Business Ownership {#sec-results-block3}

Ownership with employees pays more than paid employment but brings more volatile income, which raises the question of who takes on that risk. Unconditional entry rates are 1.99 per cent a year for the native-born, 2.22 per cent for MES migrants, 2.13 per cent for high-English MNES migrants and 1.11 per cent for low-English MNES migrants (@tbl-b3-sample). The low-English group contributes only 3,332 at-risk person-pairs and 37 entries, partly because its weak panel persistence (@tbl-panel) excludes many of its members from a sample that requires consecutive observations.

