@tbl-b3-main reports logit estimates of @eq-transition, adding regressors in steps across the four columns. Weak labour market positions are the strongest predictors of entry.[^odds] Relative to employees who are not low-paid, unemployed individuals have 2.18 times the odds of entering ownership ($\beta = 0.777$ in the *Origin and labour market* column), those outside the labour force 1.80 times and employees in the bottom wage quintile 1.50 times. Liquidity-constrained individuals are modestly more likely to enter (odds ratio 1.16). These coefficients change little across the last three columns, and adding education and employer tenure lowers the unemployment coefficient only from 0.777 to 0.700. None of the origin coefficients is significant in any column. The low-English coefficient is the largest in magnitude (-0.256 in the last column), but its standard error of 0.21 means that the data cannot distinguish large differences from none. Entry also rises with qualifications and falls with tenure with the current employer (@tbl-b3-full), which suggests that push and pull motives operate together.

[^odds]: The odds ratio is $\exp(\beta)$, the multiplicative change in the odds of entry for the given category relative to the reference category. For example, $\exp(0.777) = 2.18$, so the odds of entry are 118 per cent higher for the unemployed than for employees.



These positions can explain migrant entry only if migrants are more likely to be in them, so @tbl-b3-push-shares compares the origin groups among people at risk of entry. Holding age, sex and year fixed, low-English migrants are more likely than the native-born to be unemployed (by 1.2 percentage points, $p = 0.010$), outside the labour force (by 24.7 points, $p < 0.001$) and in the bottom wage quintile (by 2.2 points, $p = 0.040$), and somewhat more likely to be liquidity constrained (by 3.8 points, $p = 0.077$). The same pattern appears among those who do enter. Among low-English entrants, 13.5 per cent were unemployed in the year before entry and 24.3 per cent were in the bottom wage quintile, compared with 5.2 and 14.5 per cent of native-born entrants, although these shares rest on only 37 low-English entries.

### Heterogeneity by Origin {#sec-block3-het}

@tbl-b3-het allows the push from unemployment and low wages to differ by origin (@eq-transition-het). For the native-born, unemployment raises the odds of entry by a factor of 1.87 and low wages by 1.44. The push is at least as strong for every migrant group. For low-English migrants, unemployment raises the odds of entry by a factor of 7.87, which is 4.21 times the native-born push ($p = 0.005$). For MES migrants, low wages raise the odds by a factor of 2.26, 1.57 times the native-born push ($p = 0.002$), and for high-English migrants the push from unemployment is 1.40 times the native-born push ($p = 0.061$). The six interactions are jointly significant ($p = 0.001$). The low-English estimate is large but rests on only 5 entries among unemployed low-English migrants, so its confidence interval is wide.


